<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<configuration>
  <!--
    The sample restores from a local folder fed by `dotnet pack`, not from
    nuget.org. That is deliberate: restoring the last *published* version would
    test what already shipped, whereas the point here is to catch a packaging
    defect in what is about to ship — a missing dependency group, an absent XML
    doc, a type that is public in source but unreachable from outside the
    assembly.
  -->
  <packageSources>
    <clear />
    <add key="local" value="../artifacts" />
    <add key="nuget.org" value="https://api.nuget.org/v3/index.json" />
  </packageSources>

  <!--
    Listing the local feed first is not enough to guarantee it is the one used.
    A package's identity is id+version, so once a DataNet package is declared at
    a version that is also on nuget.org, restore is free to serve the published
    one — and will, silently, if it is already extracted in the global packages
    folder. That reopens exactly the failure this file exists to prevent: the
    gate would validate what already shipped while appearing to validate what is
    about to.

    Mapping pins it by identity rather than by luck: DataNet.* may come only from
    ./artifacts, so a package that was not just packed fails to restore instead
    of resolving to a stale twin. The CI job additionally gives this restore its
    own NUGET_PACKAGES, because the global folder is consulted ahead of any
    source and no mapping can override it. See
    docs/decisions/0009-sample-consumes-a-local-feed.md.
  -->
  <packageSourceMapping>
    <packageSource key="local">
      <package pattern="DataNet.*" />
    </packageSource>
    <packageSource key="nuget.org">
      <package pattern="*" />
    </packageSource>
  </packageSourceMapping>
</configuration>
